<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">North Macedonia</h1><p class="st-meta">1991 CE – present · capital Skopje</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked Balkan republic that seceded peacefully from Yugoslavia in 1991, took the name North Macedonia in 2019 and joined NATO in 2020.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The republic became independent after a referendum endorsing independence from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia</a>; it celebrates 8 September 1991 as Independence Day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> was the first country to recognise it, on 15 January 1992. It had been proclaimed in December 1944 as the People's Republic of Macedonia, one of six Yugoslav republics.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Macedonia remained at peace in the 1990s but was destabilised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> War of 1999, when about 360,000 ethnic Albanian refugees arrived. Between February and August 2001 the government fought ethnic Albanian insurgents; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> Agreement gave the Albanian minority more political power and cultural recognition. The country joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1993. Its accession to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> was long blocked by disputes with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Under the Prespa agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, signed in 2018, the country became the Republic of North Macedonia on 12 February 2019. It joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in March 2020 as the 30th member, and EU accession talks officially began in July 2022. Hristijan Mickoski became prime minister in 2024, the year Gordana Siljanovska-Davkova took office as the first female president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/north-macedonia.jpg" alt="North Macedonia" width="330" height="165"><figcaption>Image: SKopp, redrawn by Gabbe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_North_Macedonia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1991 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Skopje</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nikola Gruevski, Hristijan Mickoski, Gordana Siljanovska-Davkova</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Macedonian, Albanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Macedonia, Macedonia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=north-macedonia" aria-label="North Macedonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5201 683 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where North Macedonia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5521 939L5488 850"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5488 850h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5521 939h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5521 939h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Skopje. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=north-macedonia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
